const pikobuyArticle=`<main class="page seo-page"><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH NOTE · PIKOBUY WORKFLOW</p><h1>PikoBuy QC, warehouse and shipping: what to check before you submit a parcel</h1><p class="lead">A practical, evidence-led reading of the steps shown in PikoBuy’s public guide, shipping estimator and published service terms. It is not a price, timing or delivery guarantee.</p><article><p>When a buying-and-forwarding site is used well, the important decisions are not made in one click. They happen in sequence: identify the exact item, record the chosen variant, make the product payment, review what arrives at the warehouse, then decide whether and how to send a parcel. PikoBuy’s public beginner guide presents that same sequence. It says users can search with keywords or paste a product link, choose specifications such as colour, size and quantity, complete the first payment, review warehouse inspection information, then choose a route and pay international shipping. The useful lesson is simple: do not treat a product card, an early price, or a warehouse photo as the final answer to every later question.</p><p>This guide focuses on the parts a careful buyer can actually verify on the live PikoBuy pages. Product availability, seller policies, route availability, fees, customs treatment and delivery timing can change. Check the live order and parcel pages before confirming any payment.</p></article><article><h2>1. Start with a record that identifies the exact item</h2><p>The official guide says to find an item on marketplaces or through PikoBuy search, then confirm its specifications, colour and size before submitting a purchasing order. That is more important than it sounds. A product title can cover several variants, a seller can change pictures, and one size label may not describe the same measurements across brands. Before you pay, save the product link, seller name, selected colour, size, quantity and the listing’s measurement information. If the listing provides a size chart, compare its garment measurements with an item you already own and understand; do not assume that an S, M or L label is universal.</p><p>PikoBuy’s public guide confirms that colour, size and quantity are selected during order submission, but it does not replace the seller’s own size chart with a platform-wide sizing standard. For that reason, treat the listing chart, material description and photographs as the source for fit. If a measurement or variant is unclear, resolve it before the first payment rather than hoping a later warehouse photo will answer a missing sizing question.</p></article><article><h2>2. Understand that there are two payment stages</h2><p>The beginner guide separates the first payment for the purchasing order from the later payment for international shipping. It also notes that prices are subject to the actual purchase and says an out-of-stock order will be refunded. That means the product-side total and the final parcel-side cost should be considered separately. A product price does not establish the cost of the finished shipment.</p><p>PikoBuy’s public homepage displays payment-method logos including Visa, Mastercard, American Express and JCB. Treat those logos as a checkout prompt, not as proof that every card, country or transaction will be accepted in every case. Before paying, inspect the live checkout amount, currency, any stated fee, and the selected order details. Keep a copy of the confirmation page for your own records. Do not publish or rely on a claimed fixed exchange rate, fixed fee, or universal payment option unless it is shown in your own live checkout.</p></article><article><h2>3. Use warehouse QC as confirmation, not as a substitute for the listing</h2><p>PikoBuy describes its warehouse stage as check-in, photo confirmation and defect checking after an item arrives. Its shipping terms also say forwarded goods are unpacked and inspected, photos are provided for inspection, and extra detailed photos may be purchased. The homepage separately states that items receive quality inspection and repacking on arrival. These are useful service descriptions, but they do not turn QC into a guarantee that an item will meet every subjective expectation.</p><p>A sensible QC review compares a small list of facts you saved earlier: the product title and link, the colour and size you chose, visible logos or design details that matter to you, and any obvious condition issue. Read the photo set at full size when available. If something is important but cannot be seen, use the live order options to ask for the available additional detail rather than guessing. PikoBuy’s terms say professional inspection cannot be provided for special or professional products, so do not write an article that promises specialist testing, authenticity verification, performance testing or material certification where the platform has not said it offers those services.</p></article><article><h2>4. Know what the warehouse stage can and cannot solve</h2><p>Warehouse receipt creates a decision point, not an automatic shipping obligation. The published forwarding process says received goods are photographed for confirmation and then stored; the order page is where users locate the forwarded goods and submit a parcel. The beginner guide says users can add requests such as minimal packaging or reinforced packaging. Those options should be checked in the live parcel flow because availability can depend on the item and route.</p><p>For an item that does not match the selected variant or arrives with a visible issue, act while it is still in the warehouse and use the order page’s available after-sales path. PikoBuy’s returns policy says a returnable item generally needs a seller return guarantee, resale condition, and no more than five days in the warehouse; its application guidance states the request window is five days after the status becomes “In Warehouse.” There are exclusions and seller-dependent outcomes. In other words, warehouse photos can help you make a timely decision, but they do not guarantee that every product can be returned or that a seller will accept every request.</p></article><article><h2>5. Estimate shipping with parcel data, not product guesses</h2><p>PikoBuy’s public shipping estimator asks for destination country or region, product type, weight, length, width and height. Its beginner guide likewise says shipping routes differ in delivery time and billing methods. This is a clear warning against presenting a single “PikoBuy shipping price” in an article. A credible estimate needs the destination and parcel measurements; the result should be treated as an estimate until the live parcel page shows the available route and payment amount.</p><p>Before submitting a parcel, check the destination, the route description, the weight and dimensions entered or displayed, restricted-item information, and the final charge. Keep product discovery separate from shipping selection: a product that looks small in a photo may still require packaging, and a parcel may be billed under the route’s own method. Avoid giving readers invented delivery-day promises. PikoBuy says tracking information becomes available within three days after dispatch in its guide, while also noting that international shipping takes longer and routes differ. Tracking availability is not the same as delivery completion.</p></article><article><h2>6. Treat logistics and customs risk honestly</h2><p>PikoBuy’s shipping terms say parcels are carried by third-party logistics providers and that cross-border risks exist. The terms specifically mention customs policies and factors such as confiscation, damage, loss and peak-season delays as risks that are not fully controlled by the platform. This should change how a useful SEO article is written. Do not imply that a route is risk-free, that customs clearance is guaranteed, or that insurance removes every possible loss. Read the live route information, follow applicable law, and choose only items that are permitted for the destination and carrier.</p><p>The published terms also say PikoBuy can provide risk alerts and improve logistics insurance, but that is not a promise that every loss will be covered. If insurance or compensation is relevant to a decision, read the current terms presented for the selected route before paying. Good research content explains the uncertainty instead of hiding it behind a confident number.</p></article><article><h2>7. A practical order-to-parcel checklist</h2><p>Use this order: first, keep the live product link and chosen colour, size and quantity together. Second, complete the product payment only after checking the listing’s own measurements and seller details. Third, when the item reaches the warehouse, compare the provided photos with the record you saved; decide quickly if a return or further photo request may be needed. Fourth, build the parcel and review packaging options available in the live flow. Fifth, use the shipping estimator and parcel page with the actual destination, product type, weight and dimensions. Finally, pay shipping only after checking the route, final charge and current restrictions.</p><p>This workflow is deliberately plain. It respects what PikoBuy’s official pages say it does—search or link-based sourcing, purchasing assistance, warehouse check-in/photos/inspection, parcel submission and international shipping—without claiming more. That makes it more useful for readers and more credible for search engines than a page full of unsupported “fast,” “safe,” or “cheapest” promises.</p></article></main>`;
